.\" This document uses ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English.
.TH MINECRAFT-PING 1 "2026-07-22" "minecraft-ping" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
minecraft-ping \- measure Minecraft server latency with ping-style ergonomics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B minecraft-ping
.RI [ options ] " destination"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B minecraft-ping
measures Minecraft Java Edition and Bedrock Edition server latency while
keeping the command-line experience close to Unix
.BR ping .
Java Edition is the default.
Select Bedrock Edition with
.B --bedrock
or
.BR "--edition bedrock" .
.PP
Text mode runs as a session and prints a summary when the session ends.
JSON mode performs a single probe and emits one JSON object for scripting.
.PP
Without
.BR -- ,
options can occur before or after the destination.
After
.BR -- ,
one destination must be the final argument.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -4
Use IPv4 only.
.TP
.B -6
Use IPv6 only.
.TP
.BI "-c " count
Stop after
.I count
probes.
.TP
.BI "-i " interval
Set the minimum probe start-to-start interval to
.I interval
seconds.
This value must be positive and may be fractional.
The default is one second.
.TP
.BI "-w " deadline
Stop starting probes after
.I deadline
seconds.
This value must be positive and may be fractional.
.TP
.BI "-W " timeout
Set the socket input and output timeout to
.I timeout
seconds after each connection succeeds.
This value must be positive, may be fractional, and must not exceed 30 seconds.
The default is five seconds.
.PP
These three duration options accept decimal seconds.
The minimum is one nanosecond.
The maximum duration is 9,223,372,036.854775807 seconds.
The parser accepts exponent notation.
It rejects nonfinite values.
.B -W
has a separate maximum of 30 seconds.
.PP
.B -W
does not limit DNS work or connection setup.
.B -w
does not cancel preparation, connection setup, or an active probe.
The program checks the session deadline between probes.
.TP
.B -q
Quiet mode.
Suppress per-probe reply lines and print only the banner and summary.
.TP
.B -D
Prefix live reply lines with a Unix timestamp.
.TP
.B -n
Use numeric output only.
The first banner can contain the host name when DNS gives more than one usable
address.
.TP
.B -j
Emit a single JSON probe result.
Use this mode for scripts and automation.
.TP
.B --allow-private
Allow loopback, private, link-local, and documentation-only IP targets.
Use this only when you intentionally want to probe a local or private host.
.TP
.BR -V ", " --version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BI "--edition " kind
Select the Minecraft edition.
Supported values are
.B java
and
.BR bedrock .
.TP
.B --java
Alias for
.BR "--edition java" .
.TP
.B --bedrock
Alias for
.BR "--edition bedrock" .
.SH DESTINATIONS
The destination is positional and may be one of:
.IP \(bu 2
.I host
.IP \(bu 2
.I host:port
.IP \(bu 2
.I [ipv6]:port
.IP \(bu 2
a bare IPv6 literal such as
.I 2001:db8::20
.PP
Default ports:
.IP \(bu 2
Java Edition: 25565
.IP \(bu 2
Bedrock Edition over IPv4: 19132
.IP \(bu 2
Bedrock Edition over IPv6: 19133
.PP
If a port is present in the destination, it always wins.
.PP
One process accepts one destination.
A server name can contain no more than 253 bytes.
.SH JSON MODE
JSON mode emits one object to standard output:
.PP
.EX
{"server":"mc.example.com","latency_ms":12}
.EE
.PP
The
.B server
field contains the destination host.
The
.B latency_ms
field contains the measured latency in whole milliseconds.
.PP
The
.B -j
option is incompatible with
.BR -c ,
.BR -i ,
.BR -w ,
.BR -q ,
and
.BR -D .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Text mode got a reply and did not meet the count-and-deadline failure condition.
A JSON probe succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
Text mode got no reply.
This status also applies when count and deadline are both set and replies are
fewer than count.
A JSON probe reached the probe step and failed.
.TP
.B 2
Argv was invalid, or setup failed before a probe.
This status includes JSON preparation failures.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
minecraft-ping mc.example.com
minecraft-ping -c 3 mc.example.com
minecraft-ping --bedrock play.example.com
minecraft-ping --bedrock -6 2001:db8::20
minecraft-ping --bedrock play.example.com:19133
minecraft-ping --allow-private 127.0.0.1:25565
minecraft-ping -j mc.example.com
.EE
.SH PROTOCOL NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Java probing uses the Minecraft status and ping/pong handshake over TCP.
.IP \(bu 2
Java performs an SRV lookup only when probing
Java Edition with a hostname and no explicit port.
.IP \(bu 2
Bedrock probing uses RakNet unconnected ping/pong over UDP.
.IP \(bu 2
The tool does not fake ICMP-only fields such as
.BR ttl ,
byte counts, or
.BR icmp_seq .
.SH LIMITS
.IP \(bu 2
The tool does not log in to the server or prove that a player can join.
.IP \(bu 2
Java latency measures only the ping and pong exchange.
.IP \(bu 2
Bedrock tries resolved addresses in sequence.
.IP \(bu 2
By default, the tool rejects non-public addresses.
Use
.B --allow-private
to allow them.
.IP \(bu 2
JSON latency uses whole milliseconds and has a minimum value of 1.
.PP
See
.I docs/LIMITATIONS.md
in the source repository for the complete limits and validation boundaries.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/minecraft-ping.1
or
.IR /usr/share/man/man1/minecraft-ping.1.gz
Installed man page provided by Linux packages.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/minecraft-ping/README.md
Packaged project overview.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ping (8)
